From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 05:15:29 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] kboot: publish the APCIe tables for an OS that powers
 ANS's PCIe block back

On every post-M4 Mac ANS runs on an APCIe block whose power an OS cycles
in a deep sleep. Linux's deep sleep brings that block back by itself,
and the ten tables it needs are only in the ADT. Copy them from the APCIe
controller ANS is on (/arm-io/apcie, else /arm-io/apcie0) and its
pci-bridge0 to the DT, each as <offset mask value> records relative to
its window:

- apcie-axi2af-tunables to apple,tunable-axi2af (reg[4], "axi2af")
- apcie-phy-tunables to apple,tunable-phy (reg[2], "phy-block")
- apcie-phy-ip-pll-tunables and apcie-phy-ip-auspma-tunables to
  apple,tunable-phy-ip-pll and -phy-ip-auspma (reg[3], "phy-ip")
- apcie-cio3pllcore-tunables to apple,tunable-cio3pll (reg[5], "cio3pll")
- apcie-pcieclkgen-tunables to apple,tunable-clkgen (reg[6], "clkgen")
- the bridge's apcie-config-tunables to apple,tunable-port (reg[7],
  "port0")
- the bridge's pcie-rc-tunables, pcie-rc-gen3-shadow-tunables and
  pcie-rc-gen4-shadow-tunables to apple,tunable-rc, -rc-gen3 and -rc-gen4
  (reg[0], "config")

The host's go on the DT PCIe host whose "rc" window is the controller's
reg[1], and the bridge's on its pci@0,0. These are the names Linux's
T8140 deep sleep reads. The J700, J713, J773g, J773s and J616c ADTs all
carry the ten under these names. The J604 dump on macOS 27.0 has none, and
gets none published.

Every record must be 32 bits wide, aligned and inside its window, or no
table is published. Each table goes in with one fdt_setprop(), and if one
fails the ones already added are removed, so it is all ten or none.
Adding to the host moves its port, so the port is looked up again for each
table and for the rollback. A failure only logs. On a Mac whose ANS isn't
the post-M4 kind (no nvme-secure-bar) nothing is published.

On the Neo's live ADT (J700, September dumps) all ten pass: 274 records,
3.2 KiB of DT.

Two host-model tests compile pcie_fdt.c and kboot_boot() with their own
stubs, so they get stubs for the new code.

Signed-off-by: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
(cherry picked from commit 3729d8b1a929c4bef08cd858f7487be927b7cab2)
[Dylan: our tree has no T6050 handoff, so only the publishing hook goes into kboot_boot(); the host tests take our stubs plus the new ones; our pcie-power test already models adt and adt_path_offset, so it gets only a NULL adt_get_property and the three new stubs.]
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
